#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15ae93ce-c4dd-5b33-b9ff-381502364213" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Time-Delayed Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462f1b1b-9444-58ff-b801-8fbbfcbcb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377461} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe9218e-9c07-5797-9e3d-2114ea136be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{5{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377462} {:answer/id #uuid "1c30168a-f94d-52a2-9a98-bf5ae26c54ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377463} {:answer/id #uuid "5d96461b-b5bf-5858-b6b1-4e9dc9828ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} {:answer/id #uuid "b67556e4-5189-5cc7-aaee-81b6db402e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d96461b-b5bf-5858-b6b1-4e9dc9828ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377464} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4669c7-fc85-5f41-a5f5-f8f6933fbb16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e512d666-26fd-5562-9dad-5a955b3c727f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345242" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}+9y} & =L{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}+9L{y} & =L{5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=3$ and $f(t)=5(t-3)^{2}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u(t-3)\\cdot (t-3)^{2}} & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L{5((t+3)-3)^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot L{5{t}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3s}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}+9L{y} & =L{5{u}_{3}(t)(t-3)^{2}} \\\\ {s}^{2}Y(s)+9Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+9)Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{10{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{3}({s}^{2}+9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15ae93ce-c4dd-5b33-b9ff-381502364213" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of a Second-Order ODE With Time-Delayed Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070cb125-df6a-5a6e-a3c1-eebd12e71978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377475} {:answer/id #uuid "f4013812-37ba-5064-ab55-54d4b4b78e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-2s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377476} {:answer/id #uuid "b3afdc87-a5c1-502d-b40f-2421236c8cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} {:answer/id #uuid "436278c2-b580-52a1-8e49-b07bf5e41e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+4s+13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377478} {:answer/id #uuid "665b8f05-7e39-58e8-bd72-72806cf6ff9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{9{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3afdc87-a5c1-502d-b40f-2421236c8cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377477} :answer-field/id #uuid "443034ac-6f38-5490-9b31-4ff0cd1c4442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c8533a8-1f90-5b54-be02-41e34f166ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-345255" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y=6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1)),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+13y} & =L{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}+4L{{y}^{′}}+13L{y} & =L{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the first derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of the second derivative and the initial conditions $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0$:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand side, we use the second shifting theorem:\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)\n$$\n\nHere, $a=1$ and $f(t-1)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))$, so $f(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$ The transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)}=6\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nApplying the shift, we get\n\n$$\nL{6{u}_{1}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-1))}={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{18}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+4sY(s)+13Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ ({s}^{2}+4s+13)Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{18{e}^{-s}}{({s}^{2}+9)({s}^{2}+4s+13)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0459cf03-27db-5198-ab3a-1dbeecb675a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Initial Value Problem With Step Function Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35b6c1e-f747-5862-8863-fe053c5aa6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35b6c1e-f747-5862-8863-fe053c5aa6c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377482} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c74efd7-0f5a-5f39-afee-3b02924def1a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3731bc55-d35c-5b26-9516-425a707e0d99" :question/math-academy-id "q-345263" :question/problem "Find the solution $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)+y(t)={u}_{1}(t),\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+Y(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=u(t-1)\\,\\cdot \\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we solve for $Y(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({s}^{2}+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s} \\\\ Y(s) & ={e}^{-s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first use partial fraction decomposition on the algebraic part:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nSolving for the constants, we find $A=1$, $B=-1$, and $C=0$, giving:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the inverse Laplace transform. Let \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nf(t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nAccording to the second shifting theorem, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}={u}_{a}(t)f(t-a).\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{e}^{-s}(\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1})} \\\\ y(t) & ={u}_{1}(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-1)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)).\n$$"}]}
